how has the new godzilla movie helped  millie bobby brown dealing with the  pandemic  an epic film has hit the screen for only  the second time we'll get to see  godzilla and king kong battle  only this time the technology is way  better how much did cgi contribute to  creating the world of the film  why did alexander skarsgard learn sign  language what about the secret escapes  millie bobby brown and julian dennison  took while shooting on the gold coast  changing his direction no we're headed  straight for him  we're honestly so excited that millie  bobby brown is back to the big screen  and at only 17 the always busy actress  is no stranger to the monster content  world from her role on stranger things  and of course when she did the 2019  godzilla film  though this time she's really coming  into her own and we get to see her in a  more mature role  working on this film amid the pandemic  has actually been a really important  experience for the actress  through it she's been able to live in a  whole other world according to brown  the experience of working on this type  of film in particular is  really refreshing because she gets to be  immersed in a world of fantasy  and these otherworldly creations  shooting the film helped her to relieve  some of the stress that she'd been  feeling  and she was actually able to forget some  of the issues she was having in her own  life  while the film itself is of course very  dramatic our world is in such a chaotic  state right now that brown believes  people will enjoy watching it because it  can give us a break from our world and  allow us to totally invest in the  adventures of godzilla  and king kong for a couple of hours  this movie is of course centered on two  pretty impressive and monstrous  characters  so naturally the cast had to interact  with them but who were the actors  really acting opposite well it's not so  much who  but what most of the time when the  actors interacted with godzilla they  were communicating with the tennis ball  if the tennis ball was missing in action  sometimes it was actually just the first  assistant director who was holding up a  ragdoll and making some hand gestures  while not having a scene partner to act  with may seem tricky  millie bobby brown says it's actually a  really cool mystical experience  and because she has to visualize so much  on her own there's a kind of surreal  feel to the whole process shooting is  one thing but seeing the final product  can come as quite a surprise for the  actors since the energy in the movie is  so completely different to the bare and  simple settings they often found  themselves performing such intense  scenes in  what we see in the final cut is  terrifying monsters wreaking havoc and  destroying cities  but for brown her co-star godzilla  doesn't feel so intimidating  she's been able to see the character in  a different light and described him as a  beautiful creature  she has even confirmed that she is  definitely team godzilla  team god's a little square and then i'm  gonna wink at camera  one of the most interesting aspects of  godzilla vs kong is the significant  inclusion of sign language  child actor kaylee hoddle is deaf in  real life and so is her character in the  movie  the role she plays is actually quite  special because she's the only human  who's able to communicate with kong  cottle communicated that she was really  proud to be doing the film because of  the importance of having the deaf  community represented on screens  alexander skarsgard interacts with her  throughout the film and in order to be  able to communicate and develop a  stronger bond with her  skarsgard learned sign language he was  super impressed watching huddle work as  her precision and facial expressions  communicated so much  since hoddle is part of the deaf  community in real life she was able to  bring a unique authenticity because she  understands not only the language but  the culture  the performance is so special there's  some speculation that it might actually  be the one that really steals the film  she's the only one he'll communicate  with when it came to the sets  well what the cast and crew saw was  pretty different from what we see on  screen  see how empty this studio looks there  are only a few practical bits around the  set that act as markers in specific  locations  most of the movie was actually filmed on  large practical sets that had  huge green screens behind them to allow  the cgi to take over and really create  the environments  and of course monster characters to fill  the picture  the city where the third act of the  movie takes place was actually  completely designed by computers to  match the real city as closely as  possible  with a few futuristic additions to  upgrade the look a bit  not only did they recreate cities they  also adjusted and manipulated sizes of  real locations throughout hong kong so  the scale looked more realistic for kong  to move throughout the large buildings  almost as if they were giant trees  we can't talk about this film without  acknowledging these super impressive  water sequence  this was definitely a major part of that  200 million dollar budget  check out these underwater cameras and  the major contraptions that were needed  to shoot these scenes  audiences have never really seen  godzilla or kong in the water much  before  so seeing the way they interact with and  function in water brings a whole new  layer to these well-known characters  how about these explosion scenes we do  see some practical props with these  rocks but  overall it's far more calm without the  added cgi  but we would be lying if we said we  weren't totally blown away and impressed  by these performers all on their own  even without the effects there is no  doubt this is an action-packed film with  very talented performers bringing the  world to life  out the stellar young cast is julian  dennison there is no  shortage of talent from any of the age  groups for sure  just men being men you know for dennison  the project was actually really special  because it was the first time that he  really got to work with another  teenage actor since he was often the  only young actor on set in most of the  other productions he did  he and millie bobby brown automatically  had a special bond because  they both experienced the realities of  being child actors  they definitely had their fair share of  fun on set yeah  they're definitely having a good time a  lot of this laughter came from the fact  that the two often got to improvise  together when shooting  this added the naturalism and authentic  relationship we see between their two  characters  it may have taken a few more takes there  are even rumors that with all the  outtakes there's close to enough footage  to make a whole other film of just a gag  reel  the fun wasn't only reserved for the set  though the duo took advantage of  shooting on the gold coast to hit up  roller coasters on their days off  they even have their own extensive  secret handshake  sunrise sunset swimming fish remember  how we mentioned the movie cost 200  million to make  well it was quite a major project for  director adam wingard who had never  directed a film that cost more than  40 million they did shoot in australia  hong kong and hawaii so the hefty price  tag  isn't too surprising lingard was totally  ready for the challenge though and he  didn't approach the task lightly  he actually watched pretty much every  single godzilla and king kong film that  existed in preparation of directing this  one  he's expressed how necessary it was to  invest in research this way when working  on such a major franchise  after watching all the films it was the  1995 godzilla vs destroyer that really  hit him and inspired the way he wanted  to approach this one  all this background work helped winger  do justice to the movies which can  sometimes  stray into kind of weird territory when  done the right way  the films can show the monsters aren't  just monsters but characters we can  empathize and connect with  wynyard connected with the kong  character so much he actually edited  some parts of the script that he thought  were too harsh for kong because he felt  too bad directing anything harmful  towards this so-called monster  nathan are you okay  come on well let's just say this film  looks  incredible especially considering how  the godzilla and king kong movies used  to look  it's neat to know that the cast and crew  really had a great time on set together  creating such an epic film  and shows just how good all their  imagination skills are  how do you think this film will affect  the monster verse  [Music]

